					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/scorpion451" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1918720); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1918720&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608349850" alt="Scorpion451" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1918720)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/scorpion451" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1918720); return false;" >Scorpion451</a></span> <span class="odate time_1476923517 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">20 Oct 2016 00:31</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2673751">
									

<p>Trust me, this guide nails everything you learn doing creative work as a career. It's all about following, as Kalinin put it, the garden path.</p>
<p>And that first section is 100% axiom. There is no such thing as perfect. That way lies madness; I've seen it happen to good artists and flat out <em>break</em> them. It's one of the single most important things anyone interested in seriously pursuing creative arts has to learn. Bad is unacceptable. Good is adequate. Perfection is impossible.<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-369447-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-369447-1')">1</a></sup></p>
<div class="footnotes-footer">
<div class="title">Footnotes</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-369447-1"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-369447-1')">1</a>. You want proof, I'll point out the mistakes in the paintings of the great masters.</div>
</div>

